From 96ea8a432dc32d963ffa2336e67a5a544fe91851 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 14:16:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 06/35] windows: add the WHP vstate and MMIO device manager

Adds the two modules whose absence accounted for essentially all of the
krun-vmm Windows build failures: `src/vmm/src/windows/` (Vm, Vcpu, the run
loop and the instruction-emulator callbacks) and
`src/vmm/src/device_manager/whp/`.

Both are modelled on the HVF backend rather than the KVM one, because WHP is
the same shape of API: user-mode, explicit per-vCPU run loop, no in-hypervisor
device emulation, no ioeventfd and no irqfd. Devices raise interrupts through
the IrqChip (the WHP IOAPIC backend added earlier), not through an eventfd the
kernel picks up. Register and boot setup follows the x86_64 KVM path, since
that is where the architecture, rather than the hypervisor, decides.

The one thing with no counterpart in either existing backend is the
instruction emulator. A WHP I/O or MMIO exit carries only an address -- no
width, no direction, no data -- so the access has to be reconstructed by
`WHvEmulatorTry{Io,Mmio}Emulation`, which calls back into the VMM for the I/O
itself, for register access and for GVA translation. Those callbacks live in
`windows/emulator.rs`.

Places where WHP has no equivalent are named in comments rather than
approximated: CPUID filtering (a requested CPU template is refused, not
silently dropped), exception injection for unhandled MSRs, and pause/resume.
The HLT exit is the one place the WHP contract is genuinely ambiguous; the
handling is correct under both readings and says so.

Also extends krun-whp with the slice-based register accessors and
WHvTranslateGva that the emulator callbacks need.

krun-vmm on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc: 58 errors -> 48.
Host Linux (`cargo check -p krun-vmm -p libkrun`): 0 errors.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
